    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Describe your issue\n\nCurrently, Python objects are created by passing an `*args` tuple and (optionally) a `**kwargs` dict into the `tp_new` (and `tp_init`) slot calls, then CPython's `slot_tp_new` looks up the `__new__` attribute and calls it as a vectorcall, unpacking args and kwargs.\n\nAttribute lookups inside of CPython use very fast short-cuts these days, so beating that part will be hard, but avoiding the tuple and dict creation and doing a direct vectorcall into the Python function might still be worth it. Even a method vectorcall might already be faster than the current args/kwargs call chain, if we can figure out that we will be calling a Python function through `slot_tp_new`.",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "This is for plan non-cdef classes, right? \n\n> doing a direct vectorcall into the Python function might still be worth it.\n\nThere's a small danger that `__new__` doesn't match `tp_new` so we'd probably have to check that `cls.tp_new == object.tp_new` first. That would be a fairly odd and unhelpful corner case though",
